   "source": "# ML-03 — Frame Your Lane as an ML Task\n\n[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/Sardar-Mutahar/flyrank-ml-internship/blob/main/work/notebooks/w02_ml_task_framing.ipynb?flush_cache=true)\n\nThis skeleton is yours to fill. Work the sections **in order** — each one has a one-line hint. Simple words, honest numbers.\n\n> Working with an AI assistant? Tell it to read `skills/README.md` first and load the one skill this assignment names on its card."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 1. My lane as an ML task (type)\n\n*Classification, clustering, ranking, or scoring — which one, and why?*"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Section 1: Determine the ML task type for Lane 2\n",
    "# The decision from Week 1 is:\n",
    "#   \"Content reviewers have limited capacity and need a prioritized ranking of pages to review for possible refresh.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# Evaluate task types against this decision:\n",
    "\n",
    "# - classification: predicts yes/no for a single item. Not the right fit because\n",
    "#   the decision is about ordering/many items, not a binary yes/no per page.\n",
    "# - clustering: unsupervised grouping. Not the right fit because we have a\n",
    "#   specific decision (prioritize for refresh) rather than exploring unknown.\n",
    "# - ranking: ordering items relative to each other. Fits because the decision\n",
    "#   explicitly asks for a \"prioritized ranking.\"\n",
    "# - scoring: assigning a numerical score. Plausible as a means to ranking,\n",
    "#   but the core business need is the order, not the score itself.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Conclusion: **ranking** is the most appropriate formal ML framing.\n",
    "# The output is a ranked list (ranked action queue), and the metric that matters\n",
    "# is precision at the top of the list (top-K review). The score is an intermediate\n",
    "# mechanism to produce the ranking, but the task is about ordering pages by\n "# refresh opportunity, not about predicting a continuous value."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 2. Target or proxy\n\n*What would you predict? Where does that label come from — observed outcome or a defined rule?*"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Section 2: Target / proxy investigation\n",
    "# Load the raw data to inspect the target-related columns\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv('data/raw/content_refresh_anonymized.csv')\n",
    "print('Dataset shape:', df.shape)\n",
    "print()\n",
    "# trend_direction distribution\n",
    "td_counts = df['trend_direction'].value_counts()\n",
    "print('trend_direction distribution:')\n",
    "print(td_counts)\n",
    "print()\n",
    "# is_declining_label would be: trend_direction == 'down'\n",
    "declining_count = (df['trend_direction'] == 'down').sum()\n",
    "print(f'Rows with trend_direction == down: {declining_count} ({declining_count/len(df)*100:.1f}%)')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Verify: are there other columns that could serve as target?\n",
    "print('Columns that could serve as target/proxy:')\n",
    "for col in ['impressions_90d', 'clicks_90d', 'ctr', 'avg_position', 'content_age_days', 'days_since_last_update']:\n",
    "    print(f'  {col}: dtype={df[col].dtype}, nulls={df[col].isna().sum()}')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Check for leakage: trend_pct and trend_direction are the label source,\n",
    "# so they must NOT be used as features. Verify they exist but are noted as label source.\n",
    "print()\n",
    "print('NOTE: trend_direction and trend_pct are the label source (is_declining_label).',\n",
    "      'They must never be used as model features — that would be target leakage.')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "### Business concept\n\n> **Which pages deserve attention for possible refresh?**\n\nThe content team has limited time to review pages. They need to know which pages are most likely to benefit from a refresh — typically pages where search visibility has declined, where content has become stale, or where performance metrics suggest missed opportunities. The business concept is about prioritizing human review under capacity constraints."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "### ML target / proxy\n\n> **Measurable variable the model could potentially learn to rank:** `is_declining_label` defined as `trend_direction == 'down'`\n\n- **Why it is a reasonable proxy**: A declining trend in search impressions is the strongest observable signal that a page might benefit from a refresh. Pages with `trend_direction == 'down'` represent the majority (54.2%) of the inventory and are the natural candidates for the refresh prioritization the business needs.\n\n- **What it captures**: Pages whose trailing-90-day search impressions have declined relative to the prior 30-day window. These are pages where a refresh action (updating content, improving meta, refining intent match) is most likely to be considered.\n\n- **What it does NOT capture**: \n  * Pages that may need refresh for reasons unrelated to trend (e.g., thin content, structural issues, seasonal consolidation, competitor changes).\n  * Pages that are declining but will recover spontaneously without intervention.\n  * Pages that are stable or even growing but still have latent opportunities for improvement.\n  * The magnitude of opportunity — a page declining by 1% is treated the same as one declining by 50%.\n\n- **No target leakage**: `trend_direction` is an observed signal available at prediction time. It is not a product decision flag (`health_score`, `priority_score`, `action_type`), nor is it a future outcome that would only be known after the review decision. Using it as the ranking target is legitimate for decision-support purposes, though it is a proxy, not a ground-truth guarantee of refresh success."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "## 3. Success metric\n\n*One metric you can defend. What number means 'good'?*"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Section 3: Success metric for ranking task\n",
    "# For a ranking task where the business action is limited-capacity review,\n",
    "# Precision@K is the appropriate metric.\n",
    "\n",
    "# K is defined generically as: the number of pages the content team can\n",
    "# realistically review in a given cycle (e.g., a weekly or monthly refresh cycle).\n",
    "# The exact K is a policy choice, not a universal truth.\n",
    "\n",
    "# Verify Precision@K computation is supported in the repo infrastructure:\n",
    "from ml_utils import precision_at_k\n",
    "print('precision_at_k function imported successfully')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Conceptual check: if we ranked all 30,000 pages by refresh opportunity and\n",
    "# reviewed the top K, Precision@K would tell us what fraction of those top-K\n",
    "# pages actually have trend_direction == 'down' (are declining).\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv('data/raw/content_refresh_anonymized.csv')\n",
    "y_true = (df['trend_direction'] == 'down').astype(int).values\n",
    "# If we use a uniform score (random ranking), Precision@K should approximate\n    # the base rate (~54.2%). Good models should exceed this.\n    "k = 50\n    "random_scores = np.random.random(len(y_true))\n    "baseline_ap = precision_at_k(y_true, random_scores, k)\n    "print(f'Precision@{{k={k}}} with random ranking: {baseline_ap:.4f}')\n    "print(f'Base rate (declining proportion): {y_true.mean():.4f}')\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": "### Why this metric matches the real decision\n\n> **Precision@K** directly connects to the business action: content reviewers can\nonly review a limited number of pages per cycle. If we review the top K prioritized\npages, Precision@K tells us what fraction of those K pages are actually declining\n(and thus genuine refresh candidates). \n\n>A high Precision@K means the model is putting the most promising pages at the\ntop of the list, so the reviewer spends their limited time on pages most likely\nto benefit from a refresh. This matters more than overall accuracy across all\n30,000 pages, because the reviewer cannot act on all pages.\n\n>**K** is described generically as \"the number of pages the content team can\nrealistically review in a given cycle,\" because the exact capacity threshold is a\npolicy choice that the business makes, not a universal constant. The metric\ndesign acknowledges this by using a relative (top-K) rather than absolute measure."
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
